{"path":"docs/concepts/overview/kubectl/index.html","title":"The kubectl command-line tool","description":"Kubernetes provides a command line tool for communicating with a Kubernetes cluster's control plane, using the Kubernetes API.","body":{"type":"document","children":[{"type":"heading","level":1,"id":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The kubectl command-line tool"}]},{"type":"text","value":"kubectl is the primary command-line tool for communicating with a Kubernetes cluster. This page provides an overview of kubectl and its role in the Kubernetes ecosystem."},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kubernetes provides a command line tool for communicating with a Kubernetes cluster's "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/glossary/?all=true#term-control-plane","title":"The container orchestration layer that exposes the API and interfaces to define, deploy, and manage the lifecycle of containers.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"control plane"}]},{"type":"text","value":", using the Kubernetes API."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" tool communicates with your cluster through the "},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubernetes-api/index.html","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kubernetes API"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For configuration, "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" looks for a file named "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"config"},{"type":"text","value":" in the "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"$HOME/.kube"},{"type":"text","value":" directory. You can specify other "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/organize-cluster-access-kubeconfig/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"kubeconfig"}]},{"type":"text","value":" files by setting the "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"KUBECONFIG"},{"type":"text","value":" environment variable or by setting the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/organize-cluster-access-kubeconfig/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"--kubeconfig"}]},{"type":"text","value":" flag."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"role-of-kubectl","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Role of kubectl"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubectl/index.html#role-of-kubectl","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" tool is the primary interface for creating, inspecting, updating, and deleting Kubernetes objects. It complements the "},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/components/index.html","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kubernetes Components"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that run inside your cluster and the "},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubernetes-api/index.html","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kubernetes API"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that those components implement. Whether you run "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" from your laptop or from a Pod inside the cluster, it sends requests to the API server. Other clients, such as "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/client-libraries/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"client libraries"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and web dashboards like "},{"type":"link","href":"https://headlamp.dev/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Headlamp"}]},{"type":"text","value":", also communicate through the same API."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"how-kubectl-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How kubectl works"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubectl/index.html#how-kubectl-works","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" tool connects to the API server and authenticates using the cluster, user, and context defined in your "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/organize-cluster-access-kubeconfig/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"kubeconfig"}]},{"type":"text","value":" file. When you run "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" from outside a cluster, it uses the kubeconfig file to find the API server address and credentials. When "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" runs inside a Pod (for example, in a CI/CD pipeline), it can use in-cluster authentication based on the ServiceAccount token mounted in the Pod."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you run a command, "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" translates your intent into one or more HTTP requests to the "},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubernetes-api/index.html","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kubernetes API"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The API server validates each request, applies it to the cluster state stored in "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/","title":"Consistent and highly-available key value store used as backing store of Kubernetes for all cluster data.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"etcd"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and returns the result. This means every "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" action, whether creating a Deployment or reading logs, follows the same API-driven path."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because your kubeconfig can define multiple clusters, users, and contexts, you can use "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" to switch between clusters without reconfiguring your environment. Run "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl config use-context"},{"type":"text","value":" to change the active context."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"what-you-can-do-with-kubectl","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What you can do with kubectl"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubectl/index.html#what-you-can-do-with-kubectl","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" tool supports many operations, which fall into these broad categories:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":null,"items":[{"children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manage resources"}]},{"type":"text","value":" – Create, update, and delete objects such as Pods, Deployments, and Services. Use "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl apply"},{"type":"text","value":" for declarative management from configuration files."}]},{"children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inspect cluster state"}]},{"type":"text","value":" – List and describe objects, view events, and check resource usage."}]},{"children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debug"}]},{"type":"text","value":" – View logs from containers, execute commands inside a running container, or port-forward to a Pod."}]},{"children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cluster operations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" – Drain nodes for maintenance, cordon nodes to prevent new workloads, and manage cluster configuration."}]},{"children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Script and automate"}]},{"type":"text","value":" – Format output as JSON, YAML, or custom columns using "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/jsonpath/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"JSONPath"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for use in scripts and pipelines."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For syntax, command reference, and examples, see the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"kubectl reference documentation"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"declarative-vs-imperative","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Declarative vs imperative"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubectl/index.html#declarative-vs-imperative","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For production workloads, prefer "},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/object-management/index.html","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"declarative object management"}]},{"type":"text","value":" using "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl apply"},{"type":"text","value":" with version-controlled configuration files. Declarative management helps you track changes, collaborate, and integrate with GitOps workflows. Imperative commands (such as "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl create"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl run"},{"type":"text","value":") are useful for development and experimentation, but are harder to reproduce and audit."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"extending-kubectl-with-plugins","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extending kubectl with plugins"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubectl/index.html#extending-kubectl-with-plugins","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can extend "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/extend-kubectl/kubectl-plugins/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"plugins"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that add new sub-commands. Plugins are standalone binaries that follow the "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl-<plugin-name>"},{"type":"text","value":" naming convention. The Kubernetes community maintains many plugins, and you can manage them with the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://krew.sigs.k8s.io/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Krew"}]},{"type":"text","value":" plugin manager."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"version-compatibility","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Version compatibility"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubectl/index.html#version-compatibility","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" tool supports a version skew of plus-or-minus one minor version relative to the cluster's control plane. For example, "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" v1.32 works with control planes at v1.31, v1.32, and v1.33. Using a compatible version avoids unexpected behavior. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/releases/version-skew-policy/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"version skew policy"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for details."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"what-s-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What's next"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubectl/index.html#what-s-next","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":null,"items":[{"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"kubectl reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for syntax and command details."}]},{"children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/tools/#kubectl","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install kubectl"}]},{"type":"text","value":" on your machine."}]},{"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learn about "},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/kubernetes-api/index.html","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Kubernetes API"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"kubectl"},{"type":"text","value":" uses."}]},{"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review "},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/components/index.html","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kubernetes Components"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that make up a cluster."}]},{"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explore "},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/object-management/index.html","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Object Management"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and declarative configuration."}]},{"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/releases/version-skew-policy/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"version skew policy"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for supported version combinations."}]}]}]}}
